maya: quick question — I'm building a tiny Next.js + TypeScript quickstart that demonstrates SDK parity (server vs edge vs browser). Trying to include a small LangChain + LlamaIndex example and an eval harness. Running into an auth refresh loop when the page tries to prefetch embeddings on the client. Anyone seen this pattern? :thinking_face:

sam: hmm — do you mean the client is calling /api/embed which calls Redwood SDK and then the SDK refreshes token and retries indefinitely? seen that when the refresh path triggers a redirect instead of a token rotation. can you paste the fetch code? :eyes:

maya: yep, here's the client call in the starter app:

```ts
// app/utils/embed.ts
export async function getEmbedding(input: string) {
  const res = await fetch('/api/embed', { method: 'POST', body: JSON.stringify({ input }) });
  return res.json();
}
```

and the API handler:

```ts
// pages/api/embed.ts
import { RedwoodClient } from 'redwood-sdk';
export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const client = new RedwoodClient({});
  const { input } = await req.json();
  const emb = await client.embeddings.create({ model: 'open-small', input });
  res.json(emb);
}
```

When the browser is idle it keeps re-sending the request and the server logs show repeated 401 -> refresh -> 401 loops.
jordan: is the refresh flow using a cookie-based session or bearer token? if you're using cookie refresh that triggers a redirect to /auth it can cause the fetch to get back an HTML redirect which your client then tries to post again. that loop looks like a stale cookie + client retry mismatch. :grimacing:

lee: quick mitigation: on the server handler, detect an auth redirect response and return 401 to the client without following. also add a client-side guard to avoid infinite retries, e.g. abort after 2 refresh attempts. in our starters we include this snippet:

```ts
// server utils
async function safeCreateEmbedding(client, opts) {
  try {
    return await client.embeddings.create(opts);
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.status === 302 || e.message.includes('redirect')) {
      throw new Error('auth_redirect');
    }
    throw e;
  }
}
```

sam: for SDK parity, make sure the browser flow uses the ephemeral client token path. In the starter we show two flows: server-side secret (for SSR/Edge) and ephemeral key minted by a short-lived server endpoint for client calls. that avoids running the full refresh dance in the browser. also add a small retry policy with exponential backoff and maxAttempts = 2 in the fetch wrapper.

maya: good call — I can add an example mint endpoint. question: for the LangChain/LlamaIndex example, any preference for keeping it minimal (just a retrieval + rerank) vs full chain with tool calling? I want the sample to be copy/paste-friendly for folks evaluating DX.

jordan: keep it minimal. retrieval + prompt + eval harness is enough. include one small dataset (20 docs) and a script to run evals that records latency, token cost, and semantic similarity. users can copy the harness to run on own models. we have a tiny eval harness in the repo rdx/samples/mini-eval — I'll paste the gist in the PR.

